<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal questions</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">24 answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions From Kentucky Owners</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers about the appraisal process, costs, timing and how Kentucky rules fit in — grouped by topic.</p><p class="btns"><a class="btn coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></p></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><ul class="jump"><li><a href="#q-0">The basics</a></li><li><a href="#q-1">Costs &amp; fees</a></li><li><a href="#q-2">Timing &amp; deadlines</a></li><li><a href="#q-3">Kentucky specifics</a></li><li><a href="#q-4">Working with us</a></li></ul><h2 id="q-0">The basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Appraisal is a process written into most property policies for settling disagreements about the amount of a loss. Each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and a written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>They are similar but not identical. Appraisal is usually limited to the amount of loss and is less formal, with no hearings or discovery in most cases. How a Kentucky court treats an appraisal award is a legal question for an attorney.</p></details><details><summary>What kinds of disputes fit appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Disputes over repair scope, pricing, quantities, matching, overhead and profit, and contents values are the most common. Disputes over whether a loss is covered at all generally do not fit.</p></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Most policies let either the policyholder or the insurer demand appraisal in writing once the two sides disagree on the amount of loss. Your policy controls the exact wording.</p></details><details><summary>Is the award binding?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Most policies say the award sets the amount of loss when two of the three sign it. Courts may set aside an award in limited situations, such as fraud or an appraiser who exceeded their authority, which is a question for a Kentucky attorney.</p></details></div><h2 id="q-1">Costs &amp; fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Most policies say each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire and other shared costs equally. Read your clause, because some policies word this differently.</p></details><details><summary>How are party appraisers paid?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Fee structures include hourly, flat-fee and percentage arrangements. Ask for the fee in writing before you sign anything and ask whether the structure affects impartiality under your policy.</p></details><details><summary>How much does an umpire cost?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Umpires usually bill hourly, and the total depends on how many items are disputed and whether they inspect the property. Your share is typically half.</p></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small gap?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Often not. If the difference between estimates is only a few thousand dollars, appraisal fees may eat most of any gain. Our payoff estimator can help you compare.</p></details></div><h2 id="q-2">Timing &amp; deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Simple residential appraisals can wrap up in a few weeks, while large commercial or contested claims can take several months. Scheduling the umpire and inspections is usually the biggest variable.</p></details><details><summary>How soon must I name my appraiser?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Many policies give each side about 20 days after the written demand. Check your clause, because the window varies.</p></details><details><summary>Does appraisal stop the deadline to sue?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Not necessarily. Policies set a suit limitation period, Kentucky law limits how short it can be, and appraisal may not pause it. Read the policy and talk to a Kentucky attorney if the date is near.</p></details><details><summary>Can I demand appraisal after cashing a check?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Usually yes, if the insurer paid an undisputed amount and you still disagree about the total. Be careful with any release language, and get legal advice if a payment is labeled final.</p></details><details><summary>When should I demand appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>After you have a solid competing estimate, the insurer has had a fair chance to review it, and talks have stalled. Demanding too early, before the scope is documented, can backfire.</p></details></div><h2 id="q-3">Kentucky specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Kentucky?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>The Kentucky Department of Insurance in Frankfort, part of the Public Protection Cabinet. It accepts consumer complaints about claim handling.</p></details><details><summary>Does Kentucky law require insurers to offer appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Appraisal rights come from the policy itself. Most Kentucky property policies include an appraisal clause, but the wording varies, so read yours.</p></details><details><summary>What if my insurer is the Kentucky FAIR Plan?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>The Kentucky FAIR Plan is the property insurer of last resort. Its policy forms have their own conditions, so check whether yours includes appraisal and how it reads.</p></details><details><summary>Do you work outside Louisville and Lexington?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. We serve all 120 Kentucky counties, from Paducah and Hopkinsville in the west to Pikeville and Ashland in the east.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal resolve a bad-faith claim?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>No. Appraisal sets the amount of loss only. Questions about claims handling under Kentucky’s Unfair Claims Settlement Practices Act belong with a Kentucky attorney.</p></details></div><h2 id="q-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Neither. We are independent appraisers who handle appraisal representation, estimate review and umpire work. We do not give legal advice or negotiate coverage.</p></details><details><summary>Do you work for insurers as well as owners?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>We may serve as a party appraiser for either side or as a neutral umpire. We disclose relationships that bear on impartiality before accepting an assignment.</p></details><details><summary>What do you need to review my claim?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>The full policy with declarations and endorsements, the insurer’s estimate, any contractor bids, photos and the key correspondence. A summary of the timeline helps too.</p></details><details><summary>Can you review an estimate without starting appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. A second-look estimate review can show where the numbers differ and whether appraisal is likely to be worth it.</p></details><details><summary>How do I get started?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Use the contact page to send a short description of the loss and the dispute. We will tell you honestly whether appraisal appears to fit.</p></details></div></article>
<aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Free claim conversation</h2><p>Tell us what the carrier offered. We’ll say plainly whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn coral full" href="/contact-us/">Send claim details</a><small><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 6v6l4 2"/></svg> Mon–Fri 8am–6pm ET</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="box-links"><li><a href="/kentucky-appraisal-process/">Appraisal process guide<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal-questions/" aria-current="page">Appraisal questions<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-terms/">Appraisal terms<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-articles/">Guide articles<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="strip"><div class="wrap strip-in reveal"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re glad to talk it through.</p></div><p class="btns"><a class="btn coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Send the estimate</a></p></div></section>
